Greek police have been accused of  beating up migrants and illegally  deporting them back to Turkey reports  that Greek security forces physically  assaulted migrants emerged earlier this  year when thousands gathered in border  areas after turkey encouraged them to  leave to Europe but now DW and other  investigative reporters have been able  to confirm some sort stories of abuse  and illegal deportations after tracking  down victims in Turkey they allege Greek  police arrested them without a warrant  and forced them onto boats back to  Turkey  now Florian Schmitz is one of the  co-authors of that report and joins us  now from the Greek city of Thessaloniki  Florian what have you been hearing from  the migrants who say they've been beaten  by Greek police together with our  partners like us reports delicate and  the Dutch newspaper fell we were able to  locate migrants in Turkey who basically  told us that they were arrested by Greek  police under false pretenses saying that  they would be issued new papers and then  they were taken in almost all of them  say that the police confiscated their  phones and other personal belongings  some told us that there was violence  involved and then they say that they  were brought in a vent to the Everest  the river between Turkey and Greece  where they were put on dinghies and  pushed back to the Turkish side but at  the end of March our Turkey  correspondent Julia Hahn interviewed  refugees at the Turkish border Don of a  journey and found people with some  similar stories  that's just briefly listening we're from  Afghanistan and we've been working here  in Turkey for three to four years our  situation is very bad here we crossed  into Greece in order to go to Europe we  waited for three to four days at the  border and then we headed to  Thessalonica there the Greek police beat  us and sent us back here the Greek  police took off all our clothes last  night we only had boxer shorts again and  again  I'm telling you they told us to lay down  like that when we did they beat and then  they hit us again floor and those are  pretty strong accusations has there been  any official response from the Greek  government we did get a response the  government of course denies everything  they call the allegations regarding  human rights violations by Greek law  enforcement personnel fabricated and  false interestingly though they also say  that the measures taken so far have been  proportionate to the gravity of the  situation Greek media speak of a new  agenda of aggressive surveillance the  Conservative government here is under a  lot of pressure to manage the migrant  situation in Greece  many Greeks feel like the country is  doing the dirty work for Europe  maintaining the infamous hot spots on  the islands and thus support the  government even with such drastic  measures like push backs would also be  wrong to scapegoat Greece in the  situation since Brussels has not managed  to come up with a sustainable solution  for the migrant situation a Florida and  Ebola situation was difficult enough  before the coronavirus what impact has  the pandemic had on the situation there  the question is much rather how does  Corona affect the situation within the  country the border between between  Greece and Turkey is officially closed  all official deportation procedures are  on hold at the same time the corona  lockdown and the strict measures imposed  by the state of Greece made it a lot  easier for the police to extend into the  mainland and arrest migrants in refugee  camps and in the cities so according to  the people we talked to the corona  lockdown initiated  a new strategy of push backs from Greece  to Turkey Florent remains one of the  authors of this research thank you and  you can find the full report on our  website that's of course DW com
 

